<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the bundle's own Info.plist by the Tauri bundler, which reads
  `src-tauri/Info.plist` when it exists and adds these keys to the ones it
  generates. Nothing else in the bundle is described here — the identifier, the
  version, the minimum system version and the document types all stay in
  `tauri.conf.json`, which is the file that owns them.

  These four strings are the sentences macOS puts INSIDE its own permission
  sheet, above the Allow and Don't Allow buttons. Without them the sheet shows
  the app's name and no reason at all, which is the shape a person reads as
  "something is asking for my files" rather than as their own server asking to
  list a folder (operator's report, 2026-09-14).

  Two things about their scope are worth knowing, because both are easy to
  misread as bugs:

  - There is no notifications key, and there is no such key to add. That prompt
    is raised by `UNUserNotificationCenter` and carries no usage description;
    what explains it is the assistant's own permissions screen, which is why
    that screen exists and why the Allow button lives on it.
  - These describe prompts attributed to THIS APP. Under the launchd service
    the directory picker's `readdir` runs in `subshell-server`, a plain binary
    with no bundle and no plist, so its Files and Folders prompt carries no
    description whatever we write here. The permissions screen's `files` row
    names which of the two the person will see, from `probe.supervision`.

  The text is deliberately concrete about WHEN, not just what: a sentence that
  names the moment ("when you attach it", "so you can pick where an agent
  runs") is checkable by the person reading it, and a vague one is what makes a
  legitimate prompt look like a pretext.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Subshell Server reads a photo only when you attach it to an agent.</string>
    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Subshell Server lists this folder so you can pick where an agent runs.</string>
    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Subshell Server lists this folder so you can pick where an agent runs.</string>
    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Subshell Server lists this folder so you can pick where an agent runs.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
